---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'US F1 Student Visa from Pakistan — Complete 2026 Guide',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'VisaPath.pk' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'VisaPath.pk' },
  datePublished: '2026-10-24',
};

const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How much bank balance for US F1 visa from Pakistan?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'You must show funds equal to the first year total cost on your I-20 (typically $30,000–$80,000 depending on university), held in your or sponsor\'s account.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can I work on F1 student visa?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes, with restrictions. On-campus up to 20 hours/week during semesters, full-time during breaks. Off-campus work requires CPT or OPT authorization after one academic year.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is SEVIS fee?',
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'SEVIS I-901 fee is $350 USD, paid to the Student and Exchange Visitor Program before your visa interview. It funds the system that tracks international students.' } },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="US F1 Student Visa from Pakistan — Complete 2026 Guide"
  description="How Pakistani students apply for US F1 visa — I-20, SEVIS, DS-160, interview, financial requirements, OPT, STEM extension."
  schema={[article, faq]}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Home</a> ›
      <a href="/guides/" class="hover:text-brand-600">Guides</a> ›
      <span>US F1 Student Visa</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">US F1 Student Visa from Pakistan — Complete 2026 Guide</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mb-8">Last updated: October 2026 · 11 minute read</p>

    <section class="prose max-w-none">
      <p class="lead text-lg">
        The F1 visa is the standard route for Pakistani students pursuing degree programs in the United States. From bachelor's and master's to PhD and language programs, F1 is required for full-time academic study. This guide covers the complete process.
      </p>

      <h2>F1 vs J1 vs M1 — Which to Apply For?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>F1:</strong> academic study (bachelor's, master's, PhD, language programs)</li>
        <li><strong>J1:</strong> exchange visitor programs (research scholars, short-term)</li>
        <li><strong>M1:</strong> vocational and non-academic study</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        99% of Pakistani students apply for F1. J1 is used for Fulbright and specific exchange programs.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Get Accepted to a SEVP-Certified School</h2>
      <p>
        You can only study on F1 at institutions approved by SEVP (Student and Exchange Visitor Program). Most accredited US universities, colleges, and language schools are SEVP-certified. Verify at <code>studyinthestates.dhs.gov</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Receive Form I-20</h2>
      <p>
        After acceptance, the school issues Form I-20 — the "Certificate of Eligibility for Nonimmigrant Student Status." The I-20 contains:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your SEVIS ID (starts with N)</li>
        <li>Program dates and expected completion</li>
        <li>Total annual cost (tuition + living + fees)</li>
        <li>School information</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 3 — Pay SEVIS Fee</h2>
      <p>
        Pay the <strong>$350 SEVIS I-901 fee</strong> at <code>fmjfee.com</code>. Keep the receipt — required at your interview.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Complete DS-160</h2>
      <p>
        Fill the online nonimmigrant visa application (DS-160) at <code>ceac.state.gov/genniv</code>. Takes 60–90 minutes. Have your I-20, passport, academic history, and family details ready. Print the confirmation page with barcode.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 5 — Pay MRV Fee</h2>
      <p>
        The visa application fee is <strong>$185 USD</strong>. Pay via designated Pakistani banks or online at <code>ustraveldocs.com/pk</code>. Keep the receipt.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 6 — Schedule the Interview</h2>
      <p>
        Interviews at:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>US Embassy Islamabad</li>
        <li>US Consulate Karachi</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Student visa interviews can usually be scheduled sooner than tourist visas</strong> — the US prioritizes student slots. Even so, wait times vary by season and post.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 7 — Prepare for the Interview</h2>
      <h3>Documents to Bring</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Current passport + all previous passports</li>
        <li>Form I-20 (signed by you)</li>
        <li>DS-160 confirmation</li>
        <li>Visa interview appointment letter</li>
        <li>MRV fee receipt + SEVIS fee receipt</li>
        <li>Recent US-specification photo (2x2 inches)</li>
        <li>Academic transcripts and degree certificates</li>
        <li>English test scores (TOEFL/IELTS/Duolingo)</li>
        <li>Standardized test scores (SAT/GRE/GMAT if applicable)</li>
        <li>Bank statements showing full first-year cost</li>
        <li>Sponsor's financial documents (if parents are funding)</li>
        <li>Sponsor's affidavit of support (optional but helpful)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Financial Requirements</h2>
      <p>
        You must prove you can pay for <strong>at least the first year's total cost</strong> as stated on your I-20. For most US universities:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Public universities: $30,000–$50,000/year total</li>
        <li>Private universities: $60,000–$90,000/year total</li>
        <li>Community colleges: $15,000–$25,000/year total</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Fund sources must be clear:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Personal savings (bank statements)</li>
        <li>Parent/relative sponsorship (their statements + Affidavit of Support)</li>
        <li>Scholarship/assistantship (letter from university)</li>
        <li>Loans (approval letter)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Sudden large deposits before application look suspicious. Funds should be in the account for 2–3 months with consistent history.
      </p>

      <h2>The F1 Interview</h2>
      <p>
        Interviews are 2–5 minutes. The officer decides based on:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Are you a genuine student?</li>
        <li>Do you have ability to pay?</li>
        <li>Will you leave after your studies?</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Common Questions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Why have you chosen the United States?</li>
        <li>Why this specific university?</li>
        <li>What will you study? What courses?</li>
        <li>Did you apply to other universities?</li>
        <li>How are you funding your education?</li>
        <li>What are your plans after graduation?</li>
        <li>Why not study in Pakistan?</li>
        <li>Do you have relatives in the US?</li>
        <li>What is your undergraduate GPA?</li>
        <li>What was your TOEFL/IELTS/GRE score?</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Answering About Future Plans</h3>
      <p>
        The 214(b) presumption of immigrant intent is tricky for students. You need to show you'll return to Pakistan after studies while also demonstrating ambition that an American degree would enable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Good answer framing: "After my master's, I plan to use my OPT period to gain experience in [field]. My long-term goal is to return to Pakistan and apply these skills in [specific industry/role/opportunity in Pakistan]."
      </p>

      <h2>Visa Issuance</h2>
      <p>
        If approved, the officer keeps your passport. The F1 visa (counterfoil) is affixed and your passport returned via courier in 5–10 working days. The visa is typically issued for the full program duration shown on I-20.
      </p>

      <h2>Entering the US on F1</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You can enter the US no more than <strong>30 days before</strong> the program start date on your I-20</li>
        <li>Carry I-20, passport, and SEVIS fee receipt in hand luggage</li>
        <li>CBP officer at port of entry stamps your passport "D/S" (Duration of Status)</li>
        <li>D/S means you can stay as long as you maintain student status</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Working on F1</h2>
      <h3>On-Campus Employment</h3>
      <p>
        Allowed from day one — up to 20 hours/week during semesters, full-time during breaks. Common roles: library assistant, research assistant, teaching assistant, dining hall.
      </p>

      <h3>CPT (Curricular Practical Training)</h3>
      <p>
        Internships or paid work directly tied to your academic program. Available after one academic year of full-time study. Authorized by your Designated School Official (DSO).
      </p>

      <h3>OPT (Optional Practical Training)</h3>
      <p>
        12 months of full-time work authorization after graduation. Must be in your field of study.
      </p>

      <h3>STEM OPT Extension</h3>
      <p>
        If your degree is in STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics), you can extend OPT by 24 months — total 36 months of post-study work. This is why Pakistani students strongly favor STEM programs.
      </p>

      <h2>Maintaining F1 Status</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Enroll full-time every semester (minimum 12 credits for undergrads, 9 for grads)</li>
        <li>Maintain satisfactory academic progress</li>
        <li>Update address with your DSO within 10 days of any change</li>
        <li>Don't work off-campus without authorization</li>
        <li>Keep passport valid at all times</li>
        <li>Travel outside US only with valid I-20 signed by DSO (within last 12 months)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Common F1 Refusal Reasons</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Insufficient funds or questionable source of funds</li>
        <li>Weak academic record for chosen program</li>
        <li>Vague answers about plans after graduation</li>
        <li>Poor English communication in interview</li>
        <li>No ties to Pakistan — appearance of immigrant intent</li>
        <li>Choosing a university that doesn't match your profile (over or under-qualified)</li>
        <li>Previous visa overstays or refusals undisclosed</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>If Refused</h2>
      <p>
        Reapplying immediately usually results in another refusal. Address what caused the first refusal:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>If funds were the issue — strengthen financial documents</li>
        <li>If credibility — practice interview, be more specific about plans</li>
        <li>If program choice — reconsider university or degree</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related Resources</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/guides/us-b1-b2-visa-pakistan/">US B1/B2 Visa Guide</a> ·
        <a href="/tools/visa-interview-questions/">Interview Question Practice</a> ·
        <a href="/tools/visa-fee-calculator/">Fee Calculator</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
